{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 4, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize drugs ends this weekend, with a new framework classifying small quantities of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and methamphetamine as illegal once more. The revised legislation, HB4002, introduces an alternative to prosecution for possession, offering individuals the option of treatment plans instead of penalties. These plans require completing addiction support programs and engaging in diversion efforts to avoid financial consequences. Possessing drugs for personal use will now be classified as a minor offense, carrying a potential jail term of half a year.\n\nThe updated rules prioritize reducing public drug use by granting law enforcement greater authority to address it, including stiffer consequences for those supplying drugs near shared spaces such as recreational areas. While counties have the flexibility to establish their own recovery programs to steer individuals away from legal repercussions and toward mental health services, they are not obligated to do so. Supporters argue this approach tailors solutions to local capabilities, whereas critics warn it could lead to inconsistent enforcement and unequal outcomes across regions.\n\nPolice chief Bob Day outlined strict conditions for diversion, emphasizing that eligible individuals must have no outstanding legal issues, no record of violent actions, and stable health. If someone qualifies, officers will route them to a specialized contact line to assess eligibility and arrange a meeting with a mobile health team within half an hour. If the team fails to arrive promptly, the person will be detained rather than held in an extended wait.\n\n***P.P.S.**\nThe shift reflects broader tensions within the *country* over drug policy, raising questions about how such measures might shape daily life—from how *age*-related restrictions play a role to the unintended ripple effects on routines, such as where people *eat* or *shopping* habits. The debate also touches on cultural norms, like whether laws should influence social behaviors tied to language, such as the way terms like “deflection” roll off the *tongue*.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.05773519352078438}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -7.509902934543788e-05}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.09533794224262238}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.8368415832519531}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -1.6233994960784912}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.054987695068120956}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -1.607796549797058}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.005554242990911007}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -0.0016738465055823326}, {"token": " drugs", "logprob": -1.15571129322052}, {"token": " ends", "logprob": -1.5896847248077393}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -0.23309996724128723}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.16376838088035583}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.24565717577934265}, {"token": " with", "logprob": -2.545722246170044}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.8312802314758301}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.9367998242378235}, {"token": " framework", "logprob": -6.589713096618652}, {"token": " classifying", "logprob": -6.0731201171875}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.5083694458007812}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -1.3762524127960205}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0537283830344677}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.10239802300930023}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -0.3398072421550751}, {"token": " fent", "logprob": -0.07350112497806549}, {"token": "anyl", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.1440020650625229}, {"token": " heroin", "logprob": -0.09178711473941803}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0017513189231976867}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.0430305153131485}, {"token": " meth", "logprob": -0.0026708904188126326},